H.add([
{id:'un-founded', y:1945.8, d:'1945–1948', r:'Worldwide', c:['idea','economy'],
 t:'The United Nations and the new world order',
 s:'The victors create the United Nations, the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and a new global economic system.',
 b:`Determined not to repeat the failures of the League of Nations, the Allies founded the United Nations in San Francisco in October 1945, with 51 member states (there are now 193). Its General Assembly gives every country one vote. Its most powerful body, the Security Council, can authorise sanctions and military force, but its five permanent members (the US, the Soviet Union, now Russia, Britain, France and China) each have a veto, which means the UN cannot act against any of them or their close allies. Specialised agencies deal with health (WHO), food, children (UNICEF), refugees and culture.

At Nuremberg (1945–46), surviving Nazi leaders were tried by an international tribunal for "crimes against peace", "war crimes" and the new category of "crimes against humanity". The principle that "following orders" is no excuse, and that leaders can be held personally responsible, became a foundation of international law. Similar trials were held in Tokyo. In 1948 the UN adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, drafted by a committee chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt, and the Genocide Convention.

The economic order was also rebuilt. At Bretton Woods (1944), the Allies created the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank, and fixed currencies to the US dollar, which was tied to gold. The General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (1947, later the World Trade Organization) worked to reduce barriers to trade. The aim was to avoid the economic nationalism of the 1930s. These institutions, largely designed by the US, are often called the "liberal international order".`,
 m:'Most of the international institutions and rules that govern the world today, imperfect as they are, were created in these few years in response to the catastrophe of the World Wars.',
 p:['Franklin D. Roosevelt','Eleanor Roosevelt','John Maynard Keynes','René Cassin'], l:['versailles','wwii-europe','holocaust','cold-war-begins'], k:['human-rights','sovereignty','genocide','globalization']},

{id:'cold-war-begins', y:1947, d:'1945–1949', r:'Europe → worldwide', c:['war','idea'],
 t:'The Cold War begins: the Iron Curtain, the Marshall Plan and NATO',
 s:'The wartime alliance between the US and the Soviet Union collapses into a global rivalry that divides Europe and the world.',
 b:`The US and the USSR had been allies against Hitler, but they had opposite systems and deep mutual suspicion. As the Red Army had liberated Eastern Europe, Stalin installed communist governments there (Poland, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, Romania, Bulgaria, East Germany), wanting a buffer zone after two German invasions. In 1946 Winston Churchill declared that "an iron curtain has descended across the continent".

In 1947 President Harry Truman announced the "Truman Doctrine": the US would support free peoples resisting communist subversion, a policy of "containment". The US launched the Marshall Plan, giving about $13 billion (well over $150 billion today) to rebuild Western Europe's economies, partly so that poverty would not drive people toward communism. The Soviets refused it for their bloc.

Germany was divided into occupation zones. In 1948 Stalin blockaded West Berlin, deep inside the Soviet zone, to force the Western powers out. The Americans and British responded with the Berlin Airlift, flying in food and coal for almost a year until Stalin gave up. In 1949 Germany split into West Germany (democratic, capitalist) and East Germany (communist). The same year, Western nations formed NATO (the North Atlantic Treaty Organization), a military alliance in which an attack on one is an attack on all. The USSR tested its first atomic bomb in 1949, and in 1955 formed the Warsaw Pact. The world was divided into two blocs, with many newly independent countries later trying to stay "non-aligned".`,
 m:'The Cold War shaped world politics for over 40 years. NATO, the division of Europe, and many conflicts of the era came from this moment.',
 p:['Harry Truman','Joseph Stalin','Winston Churchill','George Marshall','George Kennan'], l:['wwii-europe','un-founded','korean-war','cuban-missile-crisis','fall-of-berlin-wall'], k:['communism','capitalism','geopolitics','nuclear-deterrence']},

{id:'india-partition', y:1947.6, d:'1947', r:'India & Pakistan', c:['war','migration','revolution'],
 t:'Indian independence and the Partition',
 s:'British India becomes independent as two countries, India and Pakistan; the partition triggers mass violence and one of history\'s largest migrations.',
 b:`By the 1940s, the independence movement was split. Muhammad Ali Jinnah's Muslim League argued that Muslims would be a permanent minority in a Hindu-majority India and demanded a separate homeland, Pakistan. Gandhi and Nehru's Congress wanted a united India. Communal violence between Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs grew, especially after the "Direct Action Day" riots in Calcutta in 1946.

Exhausted by war, Britain decided to leave quickly. The last viceroy, Lord Mountbatten, brought forward the date of independence. A British lawyer, Cyril Radcliffe, who had never been to India, was given about five weeks to draw borders dividing the provinces of Punjab in the west and Bengal in the east. Pakistan was created in two parts, West and East, separated by about 1,600 km of India. At midnight on 14–15 August 1947, Nehru declared that India was awakening "to life and freedom".

What followed was catastrophe. About 12–15 million people crossed the new borders, Hindus and Sikhs fleeing to India and Muslims to Pakistan, in one of the largest migrations in history. Trains arrived filled with corpses. Estimates of the dead range from 200,000 to two million, and tens of thousands of women were abducted and raped. The princely state of Kashmir, with a Muslim majority but a Hindu ruler, joined India, triggering the first war between India and Pakistan; Kashmir remains disputed. In 1971 East Pakistan broke away, after a brutal war, to become Bangladesh. India became the world's largest democracy; Pakistan experienced repeated military rule. Both became nuclear powers.`,
 m:'Partition created India, Pakistan and later Bangladesh, home today to about a quarter of humanity, and left the Kashmir conflict and India-Pakistan hostility unresolved.',
 p:['Jawaharlal Nehru','Muhammad Ali Jinnah','Lord Mountbatten','Mohandas Gandhi','Sardar Patel'], l:['gandhi-independence','british-raj','delhi-sultanate','african-decolonization'], k:['nationalism','imperialism','sovereignty']},

{id:'israel-founded', y:1948, d:'1897–1949', r:'Israel / Palestine', c:['war','migration','revolution'],
 t:'The founding of Israel and the Nakba',
 s:'Israel declares independence in 1948; the war that follows creates a Jewish state and makes some 700,000 Palestinian Arabs refugees.',
 b:`In the late 19th century, facing persecution and pogroms in Europe, Jewish thinkers like Theodor Herzl founded Zionism, the movement for a Jewish national home in the ancestral land of Israel, then the Ottoman province of Palestine, inhabited mainly by Arabs. Jewish immigration grew, especially after the Balfour Declaration (1917) and the British Mandate. Palestinian Arabs, who also had growing national aspirations, increasingly opposed it, leading to riots and an Arab revolt (1936–39). Britain then restricted Jewish immigration just as Jews were trying to escape Nazi Europe.

After the Holocaust, international sympathy for a Jewish state grew. In November 1947 the UN voted to partition Palestine into a Jewish state and an Arab state, with Jerusalem under international control. Jewish leaders accepted; Arab leaders rejected it, arguing that the majority Arab population was being denied self-determination. Fighting erupted. On 14 May 1948, as the British left, David Ben-Gurion declared the State of Israel. The next day, the armies of Egypt, Jordan, Syria, Iraq and Lebanon invaded.

Israel won the war and ended up with more territory than the UN plan proposed. Jordan took the West Bank and East Jerusalem; Egypt took the Gaza Strip. About 700,000 Palestinian Arabs fled or were expelled from their homes, and were not allowed to return. Palestinians call this the "Nakba" ("catastrophe"). Their descendants, millions of refugees, still live in camps in Gaza, the West Bank, Jordan, Lebanon and Syria. In the following years, about 800,000 Jews left or were driven from Arab countries, most settling in Israel.`,
 m:'The creation of Israel fulfilled the Zionist dream of a Jewish homeland and created the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, one of the most enduring and divisive conflicts in the world.',
 p:['Theodor Herzl','Chaim Weizmann','David Ben-Gurion','Golda Meir','Haj Amin al-Husseini'], l:['jewish-diaspora','ottoman-collapse','holocaust','six-day-war','israel-gaza-war'], k:['nationalism','judaism','sovereignty']},

{id:'apartheid-begins', y:1948.5, d:'1948–1994', r:'South Africa', c:['movement','idea'],
 t:'Apartheid in South Africa',
 s:'South Africa\'s white minority government creates a legal system of racial segregation; a long struggle follows, led by Nelson Mandela.',
 b:`South Africa had been ruled by its white minority, descendants of Dutch ("Afrikaner") and British settlers, since colonial times. In 1948 the National Party came to power and introduced "apartheid" ("separateness" in Afrikaans): a total system of legal racial segregation. Every person was classified by race: White, Black, "Coloured" (mixed race) or Indian. Marriage between races was banned. Black South Africans, about 70% of the population, had to carry passes, were barred from "white" areas, had inferior schools and hospitals, and could not vote. Millions were forcibly removed from their homes, and the government created "homelands" (Bantustans) to strip Black people of South African citizenship.

The African National Congress (ANC) led resistance, at first through non-violent protests. In 1960, police shot dead 69 peaceful protesters at Sharpeville. The ANC was banned and turned to armed struggle. Its leader, Nelson Mandela, was sentenced to life imprisonment in 1964 and spent 27 years in prison, mostly on Robben Island. In 1976 schoolchildren in Soweto protested against being taught in Afrikaans; police killed hundreds. Activists like Steve Biko, who died in police custody in 1977, inspired a new generation.

Internationally, apartheid became a pariah system. Sports and cultural boycotts, economic sanctions and divestment campaigns isolated South Africa. By the late 1980s, facing economic crisis, internal uprisings and the end of the Cold War (which removed the government's anti-communist justification), President F.W. de Klerk changed course. In 1990 he legalised the ANC and released Mandela. In 1994, in the first elections in which all South Africans could vote, Mandela became president. His government's Truth and Reconciliation Commission, chaired by Archbishop Desmond Tutu, investigated apartheid-era crimes, offering amnesty in exchange for full confession.`,
 m:'The fall of apartheid was one of the great victories of the 20th century for human rights, and Mandela became a global symbol of reconciliation.',
 p:['Nelson Mandela','Oliver Tambo','Steve Biko','Desmond Tutu','F.W. de Klerk','Winnie Madikizela-Mandela'], l:['scramble-for-africa','gandhi-independence','civil-rights','african-decolonization'], k:['racism','human-rights','democracy']},

{id:'mao-china', y:1949.8, d:'1949–1976', r:'China', c:['revolution','disaster'],
 t:'Mao\'s China: the Great Leap Forward and the Cultural Revolution',
 s:'Mao Zedong\'s communist China pursues radical campaigns that cause the deadliest famine in history and a decade of chaos.',
 b:`When Mao Zedong proclaimed the People's Republic of China in 1949, many Chinese welcomed the end of decades of war. The Communists unified the country, ended foreign domination, improved literacy and public health, and gave women legal rights. But they also carried out violent land reform in which perhaps one to two million landlords were killed, and suppressed all opposition.

In 1958 Mao launched the "Great Leap Forward" to overtake Britain's industrial output in fifteen years. Peasants were forced into giant communes, and millions were told to make steel in backyard furnaces, melting pots and tools to produce useless metal. Officials, afraid to report failure, wildly exaggerated harvests, so the state took grain the peasants needed to survive. The result was the deadliest famine in human history (1959–61): an estimated 30–45 million people died. Officials who criticised the policy were purged.

Sidelined after the disaster, Mao struck back in 1966 with the "Great Proletarian Cultural Revolution". He called on young people to attack "bourgeois" and "revisionist" elements in the Party and society. Millions of students formed "Red Guard" units, waving Mao's "Little Red Book" of quotations. They humiliated, beat and killed teachers, officials and intellectuals, destroyed temples, books and artworks, and fought each other. Senior leaders, including Deng Xiaoping, were purged. Some 17 million young people were "sent down" to the countryside. Hundreds of thousands to perhaps two million died. The chaos only ended with Mao's death in 1976.`,
 m:'Mao\'s campaigns were among the worst human-made disasters in history, and the Communist Party\'s later turn to market reform was partly a reaction to them.',
 p:['Mao Zedong','Zhou Enlai','Liu Shaoqi','Deng Xiaoping','Jiang Qing'], l:['chinese-civil-war','korean-war','deng-reforms','rise-of-china'], k:['communism','totalitarianism']},

{id:'korean-war', y:1950, d:'1950–1953', r:'Korea', c:['war'],
 t:'The Korean War',
 s:'Communist North Korea invades the South; a war involving the US, UN and China ends in stalemate and a divided peninsula.',
 b:`Korea was a Japanese colony from 1910 until 1945. After Japan's defeat, it was divided "temporarily" at the 38th parallel: the Soviets occupied the north and the Americans the south. By 1948 two rival states had formed: the communist Democratic People's Republic of Korea in the north under Kim Il-sung, and the anti-communist Republic of Korea in the south under Syngman Rhee.

On 25 June 1950, with Stalin's approval, North Korea invaded the South and nearly conquered it. Because the Soviet Union was boycotting the UN Security Council at the time, the US was able to get UN backing for a military response, and troops from 16 countries, mostly American, fought under the UN flag. General Douglas MacArthur's daring landing at Inchon drove the North Koreans back, and UN forces advanced almost to the Chinese border. Then China intervened with hundreds of thousands of "volunteers", pushing the UN forces back south. The war became a bloody stalemate around the 38th parallel.

An armistice was signed in 1953, but no peace treaty. Around 3 million people died, most of them civilians, and much of Korea was flattened. The peninsula remains divided by a heavily fortified Demilitarized Zone (DMZ). North Korea became a closed, totalitarian dynastic state ruled by Kim Il-sung, his son Kim Jong-il and grandson Kim Jong-un, and developed nuclear weapons. South Korea, after decades of military rule, became a prosperous democracy and a technological and cultural powerhouse.`,
 m:'The Korean War was the first major "hot" war of the Cold War, and the division of Korea is one of the last remaining Cold War frontlines.',
 p:['Kim Il-sung','Syngman Rhee','Douglas MacArthur','Harry Truman','Mao Zedong'], l:['cold-war-begins','mao-china','vietnam-war'], k:['communism','geopolitics','nuclear-deterrence']},

{id:'civil-rights', y:1955, d:'1954–1968', r:'United States', c:['movement'],
 t:'The American Civil Rights Movement',
 s:'Black Americans and their allies use non-violent protest to end legal segregation and win voting rights.',
 b:`A century after the end of slavery, Black Americans in the South still lived under "Jim Crow" segregation: separate (and inferior) schools, restaurants, buses and even water fountains. Most were prevented from voting through literacy tests, poll taxes and intimidation. Lynching terrorised communities. In the North, discrimination in housing and jobs was widespread.

In 1954, in Brown v. Board of Education, the Supreme Court ruled that segregated schools were unconstitutional, the result of years of legal work by the NAACP and lawyer Thurgood Marshall. In 1955, in Montgomery, Alabama, Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat to a white man. The year-long bus boycott that followed was led by a young pastor, Martin Luther King Jr., who drew on Gandhi's methods of non-violent resistance. Students staged sit-ins at segregated lunch counters; "Freedom Riders" rode buses across the South. Protesters were beaten, jailed and murdered, and televised images of police using dogs and fire hoses on children in Birmingham (1963) shocked the nation.

In August 1963, at the March on Washington, King delivered his "I Have a Dream" speech to 250,000 people. The Civil Rights Act (1964) banned segregation and discrimination, and after the brutal attack on marchers at Selma, the Voting Rights Act (1965) protected Black voting rights. Other voices, like Malcolm X, argued for Black pride, self-defence and separatism. Malcolm X was assassinated in 1965 and King in 1968. The movement inspired others: for women's rights, Latino farm workers, Native Americans and gay rights.`,
 m:'The movement ended legal segregation in the US, became a global model for non-violent protest and human rights, though racial inequality remains a central issue in American life.',
 p:['Martin Luther King Jr.','Rosa Parks','Malcolm X','Thurgood Marshall','John Lewis','Lyndon B. Johnson'], l:['us-civil-war','gandhi-independence','second-wave-feminism','apartheid-begins'], k:['racism','human-rights','democracy']},

{id:'european-integration', y:1957, d:'1951–1993', r:'Europe', c:['idea','economy'],
 t:'The European Union: from coal and steel to a continental union',
 s:'Former enemies France and Germany lead the creation of a European community that grows into the European Union.',
 b:`After two world wars that began in Europe, leaders sought a way to make war between France and Germany "not merely unthinkable, but materially impossible", in the words of French foreign minister Robert Schuman. In 1951, six countries (France, West Germany, Italy, Belgium, the Netherlands and Luxembourg) pooled their coal and steel industries, the raw materials of war, in the European Coal and Steel Community.

In 1957 the same six signed the Treaty of Rome, creating the European Economic Community (EEC), a "common market" without tariffs between members. Its institutions included a Commission, a Council of Ministers, a Parliament and a Court of Justice. It brought rapid prosperity. Britain, Ireland and Denmark joined in 1973; Greece, Spain and Portugal in the 1980s after escaping dictatorships, with membership helping cement their democracies.

The Maastricht Treaty (1992) turned the community into the European Union (EU), with common citizenship, free movement of people, and plans for a single currency, the euro, launched in 1999–2002. After the Cold War, the EU expanded east, admitting ten countries in 2004, mostly former communist states such as Poland, Hungary and the Baltic states. The EU has faced crises: the euro debt crisis, migration disputes, and Britain's vote to leave ("Brexit") in 2016. Still, it is a unique experiment in sharing sovereignty among nations and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2012.`,
 m:'European integration turned a continent of endless wars into a zone of peace and prosperity, and shows one way nations can pool sovereignty for common goals.',
 p:['Robert Schuman','Jean Monnet','Konrad Adenauer','Jacques Delors'], l:['wwii-europe','cold-war-begins','fall-of-berlin-wall','populism-brexit'], k:['sovereignty','nationalism','globalization','democracy']},

{id:'african-decolonization', y:1960, d:'1945–1975', r:'Africa & Asia', c:['revolution','movement','war'],
 t:'Decolonisation: the end of European empires',
 s:'In three decades, dozens of colonies in Asia and Africa win independence, often after bitter struggles.',
 b:`The Second World War fatally weakened the European empires. Britain and France were exhausted and indebted; Japan had shown that colonial rulers could be beaten; colonial soldiers had fought for "freedom" and expected it; and both superpowers, the US and USSR, opposed old-style colonialism. Nationalist movements, led by Western-educated elites, demanded independence.

In Asia, India and Pakistan (1947), Burma and Sri Lanka (1948) became independent. Indonesia won independence from the Netherlands after a four-year war (1949). In Vietnam, Ho Chi Minh's communists defeated France at Dien Bien Phu (1954). In Africa, Ghana, led by Kwame Nkrumah, became the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence in 1957. In 1960, the "Year of Africa", seventeen African countries became independent. In Kenya, the British brutally suppressed the Mau Mau uprising (1952–60) with detention camps and torture, before granting independence in 1963. Algeria won independence from France in 1962 after an eight-year war that killed hundreds of thousands. Portugal held on to Angola and Mozambique until 1975, after long wars.

Independence was often followed by enormous challenges. Colonial borders lumped rival groups together, economies depended on exporting a few raw materials, and few people had been trained to govern. The Congo, granted independence by Belgium in 1960, collapsed into crisis; its elected prime minister, Patrice Lumumba, was murdered in 1961 with Belgian and US involvement. Cold War rivalries fuelled coups and civil wars. Many new states became one-party states or military dictatorships. Some leaders, such as Nkrumah, Nyerere and Nehru, joined the "Non-Aligned Movement", refusing to choose between the superpowers.`,
 m:'Decolonisation more than tripled the number of independent countries and gave billions of people self-government, while leaving legacies of poverty, conflict and arbitrary borders.',
 p:['Kwame Nkrumah','Jomo Kenyatta','Patrice Lumumba','Julius Nyerere','Sukarno','Ho Chi Minh','Frantz Fanon'], l:['scramble-for-africa','india-partition','vietnam-war','apartheid-begins','rwanda'], k:['imperialism','nationalism','sovereignty']},

{id:'cuban-revolution', y:1959, d:'1953–1959 and after', r:'Cuba & Latin America', c:['revolution','war'],
 t:'The Cuban Revolution and the Cold War in Latin America',
 s:'Fidel Castro overthrows a dictatorship and creates a communist state 150 km from the US; the Cold War transforms Latin America.',
 b:`Cuba in the 1950s was ruled by Fulgencio Batista, a corrupt dictator friendly to US business and the American mafia, which ran Havana's casinos. Most Cubans were poor while US companies owned much of the land. In 1956 a young lawyer, Fidel Castro, his brother Raúl and the Argentine doctor Ernesto "Che" Guevara landed with a small band of rebels and began a guerrilla war from the Sierra Maestra mountains. On 1 January 1959, Batista fled and the rebels marched into Havana.

Castro nationalised land and businesses, including American-owned ones, and moved toward the Soviet Union. In 1961 the CIA sponsored an invasion by Cuban exiles at the Bay of Pigs, which failed humiliatingly. Castro declared Cuba socialist. The US imposed an embargo that lasts to this day. Cuba achieved high literacy and good healthcare but became a one-party state that jailed dissidents, and over a million Cubans emigrated, mostly to Florida.

Afraid of "another Cuba", the US backed anti-communist governments and military coups across Latin America. In Chile, the socialist president Salvador Allende, elected in 1970, was overthrown in a coup on 11 September 1973 by General Augusto Pinochet, with US encouragement; thousands were killed or tortured. Argentina's military junta (1976–83) "disappeared" up to 30,000 people. Brutal civil wars in Guatemala, El Salvador and Nicaragua followed in the 1970s–80s. By the 1990s, most of Latin America had returned to democracy.`,
 m:'The Cuban Revolution brought the Cold War to the Americas and inspired revolutionaries worldwide; US interventions in response left a lasting legacy of distrust in Latin America.',
 p:['Fidel Castro','Che Guevara','Fulgencio Batista','Salvador Allende','Augusto Pinochet'], l:['latin-american-independence','cuban-missile-crisis','cold-war-begins'], k:['communism','imperialism','democracy']},

{id:'space-race', y:1957.8, d:'1957–1969', r:'USSR, US & space', c:['science','war'],
 t:'The Space Race: from Sputnik to the Moon',
 s:'The Soviet Union launches the first satellite and first human into space; the US answers by landing men on the Moon.',
 b:`On 4 October 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, a metal sphere about the size of a beach ball that orbited the Earth beeping radio signals. It was the first artificial satellite, and it shocked Americans. If the Soviets had rockets powerful enough to reach orbit, they could deliver nuclear warheads anywhere on Earth. The US responded by creating NASA (1958) and pouring money into science education.

The Soviets kept winning firsts: the first animal in orbit (the dog Laika, 1957), and on 12 April 1961 the first human in space, Yuri Gagarin, who orbited the Earth in 108 minutes. In 1963 Valentina Tereshkova became the first woman in space. The Soviet program was led by the secret "Chief Designer", Sergei Korolev.

In 1961 President John F. Kennedy committed the US to "landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely to the Earth" before the decade was out. The Apollo program employed 400,000 people. On 20 July 1969, Apollo 11 landed on the Moon. As Neil Armstrong stepped onto the surface, he said, "That's one small step for a man, one giant leap for mankind." About 600 million people watched on television. Twelve Americans walked on the Moon by 1972. The Space Race produced satellites for communication, weather forecasting and GPS, and photographs of Earth from space like "Earthrise" helped inspire the environmental movement.`,
 m:'The Space Race was Cold War competition by other means, and it produced the satellite technology that underpins modern communications, navigation and climate science.',
 p:['Sergei Korolev','Yuri Gagarin','Valentina Tereshkova','John F. Kennedy','Neil Armstrong','Buzz Aldrin','Wernher von Braun'], l:['cold-war-begins','cuban-missile-crisis','internet','climate-change'], k:['scientific-method','nuclear-deterrence']},

{id:'cuban-missile-crisis', y:1962.8, d:'October 1962', r:'Cuba, US & USSR', c:['war'],
 t:'The Cuban Missile Crisis',
 s:'For thirteen days, the US and USSR stand on the brink of nuclear war over Soviet missiles in Cuba.',
 b:`In 1962 Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev secretly began installing nuclear missiles in Cuba, just 150 km from Florida. He wanted to protect Castro from another US invasion, to answer the American nuclear missiles stationed in Turkey near the Soviet border, and to shift the nuclear balance, which strongly favoured the US.

On 14 October 1962, an American U-2 spy plane photographed the missile sites. President John F. Kennedy and his advisors debated for days. Military leaders urged air strikes and an invasion. Kennedy chose a "quarantine", a naval blockade, to stop further Soviet shipments, and demanded the missiles' removal. The world held its breath as Soviet ships approached the blockade line. On 27 October, "Black Saturday", a U-2 was shot down over Cuba, and a Soviet submarine, harassed by US warships, nearly launched a nuclear torpedo; one officer, Vasili Arkhipov, refused to agree.

Finally, through public and secret back-channel negotiations, a deal was reached: the Soviets would remove their missiles from Cuba, and the US publicly promised not to invade Cuba and secretly agreed to remove its missiles from Turkey. Both leaders had stepped back from the brink. Afterwards, a direct "hotline" was set up between Washington and Moscow, and in 1963 the two sides signed a treaty banning nuclear tests in the atmosphere.`,
 m:'It was the closest the world has come to nuclear war. The crisis taught both superpowers to manage their rivalry more carefully and led to the first arms-control agreements.',
 p:['John F. Kennedy','Nikita Khrushchev','Fidel Castro','Robert Kennedy','Vasili Arkhipov'], l:['cuban-revolution','hiroshima','cold-war-begins','space-race'], k:['nuclear-deterrence','geopolitics']},

{id:'vietnam-war', y:1965, d:'1955–1975', r:'Vietnam, Laos, Cambodia', c:['war'],
 t:'The Vietnam War',
 s:'The US fights to stop communist North Vietnam from uniting the country; it loses, at a cost of millions of lives.',
 b:`After Ho Chi Minh's Viet Minh defeated the French in 1954, Vietnam was divided at the 17th parallel: a communist North and an anti-communist South. Elections to reunify the country were supposed to happen in 1956, but the South, backed by the US, which feared Ho would win, refused. Americans believed in the "domino theory": if Vietnam fell to communism, all of Southeast Asia would follow.

The South Vietnamese government was unpopular and authoritarian, and communist guerrillas in the South, the Viet Cong, supported by the North, gained strength. The US sent advisors, then, after the disputed Gulf of Tonkin incident in 1964, combat troops. By 1968 over 500,000 American soldiers were in Vietnam. The US dropped more bombs on Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia than all sides used in the Second World War, and sprayed the chemical defoliant Agent Orange, which still causes birth defects. The enemy used jungle, tunnels and the "Ho Chi Minh Trail" through Laos and Cambodia, and could not be defeated.

The 1968 Tet Offensive, a surprise attack on cities across the South, was a military failure for the communists but a political victory, convincing many Americans the war could not be won. The massacre of villagers at My Lai by US soldiers horrified the public. A massive anti-war movement grew in the US, with protests on campuses; four students were shot dead by National Guardsmen at Kent State (1970). US troops withdrew in 1973. In April 1975 North Vietnamese tanks rolled into Saigon, and Vietnam was united under communist rule. About 58,000 Americans and perhaps 2–3 million Vietnamese died. Hundreds of thousands of "boat people" later fled.`,
 m:'The war was America\'s first clear defeat, damaged trust in government, and made Americans wary of foreign wars for decades (the "Vietnam syndrome").',
 p:['Ho Chi Minh','Ngo Dinh Diem','Lyndon B. Johnson','Richard Nixon','Henry Kissinger','Vo Nguyen Giap'], l:['african-decolonization','korean-war','cambodia-genocide','sixty-eight'], k:['communism','nationalism','imperialism','geopolitics']},

{id:'six-day-war', y:1967, d:'1956–1979', r:'Middle East', c:['war','economy'],
 t:'The Arab-Israeli wars, the oil shock and Camp David',
 s:'Israel defeats Arab armies in 1967 and occupies the West Bank and Gaza; a 1973 war triggers a global oil crisis and later a peace with Egypt.',
 b:`Egypt's charismatic leader Gamal Abdel Nasser became a hero of Arab nationalism when he nationalised the Suez Canal in 1956 and survived an invasion by Britain, France and Israel, which were forced to withdraw under US and Soviet pressure. The "Suez Crisis" marked the end of Britain as a great power.

In June 1967, after Egypt closed the Straits of Tiran to Israeli shipping and massed troops in Sinai, Israel launched a pre-emptive strike. In six days it defeated Egypt, Jordan and Syria, capturing the Sinai Peninsula and Gaza Strip from Egypt, the West Bank and East Jerusalem from Jordan, and the Golan Heights from Syria. About a million Palestinians came under Israeli military occupation, and Israel began building Jewish settlements in the occupied territories, which most of the world considers illegal under international law. The Palestine Liberation Organization (PLO), led by Yasser Arafat, became the main voice of Palestinian nationalism, and some factions turned to terrorism, such as the murder of Israeli athletes at the 1972 Munich Olympics.

On Yom Kippur, the holiest day in Judaism, in October 1973, Egypt and Syria launched a surprise attack. Israel recovered and won, but at heavy cost. In retaliation for Western support of Israel, Arab oil producers imposed an oil embargo, and oil prices quadrupled. The "oil shock" caused recession and inflation in the West and showed the power of oil-producing states. In 1977 Egyptian President Anwar Sadat made a historic visit to Jerusalem. At Camp David in 1978, mediated by US President Jimmy Carter, Egypt and Israel agreed to peace; Israel returned Sinai. Sadat was assassinated by Islamist extremists in 1981.`,
 m:'The 1967 war created the occupation at the heart of the Israeli-Palestinian conflict today; the 1973 oil shock showed how Middle Eastern politics can shake the world economy.',
 p:['Gamal Abdel Nasser','Moshe Dayan','Golda Meir','Yasser Arafat','Anwar Sadat','Menachem Begin','Jimmy Carter'], l:['israel-founded','iranian-revolution','israel-gaza-war'], k:['nationalism','terrorism','geopolitics']},

{id:'sixty-eight', y:1968, d:'1960s–1970s', r:'Worldwide', c:['movement','idea'],
 t:'1968 and the counterculture',
 s:'A global wave of youth protest challenges authority, war, tradition and conformity, from Paris to Prague to Mexico City.',
 b:`By the 1960s, a huge "baby boom" generation, born after the Second World War, was coming of age, better educated and more prosperous than any before. Many rejected the conformity, conservatism and authority of their parents' world. Rock music, the Beatles, long hair, the contraceptive pill, recreational drugs and the "hippie" counterculture symbolised the change.

In 1968 protest erupted around the world. In the US, opposition to the Vietnam War and the assassinations of Martin Luther King Jr. and Robert Kennedy led to riots and clashes. In Paris in May 1968, students occupied universities and built barricades; ten million workers went on strike, nearly toppling President de Gaulle. In Czechoslovakia, the "Prague Spring" reformist communists under Alexander Dubček tried to create "socialism with a human face", with free speech; in August, Soviet and Warsaw Pact tanks crushed it. In Mexico City, just before the Olympics, soldiers massacred hundreds of student protesters at Tlatelolco. In West Germany, students confronted their parents about the Nazi past.

The movements of the 1960s did not overthrow governments, but they transformed societies. They gave rise to or strengthened the modern environmental, feminist, gay rights and anti-war movements, and permanently loosened social attitudes toward sex, religion, authority and personal freedom. They also provoked a conservative backlash that shaped politics for decades.`,
 m:'The cultural revolution of the 1960s reshaped values in much of the world. Many of today\'s "culture wars" trace back to this era.',
 p:['Martin Luther King Jr.','Alexander Dubček','Daniel Cohn-Bendit','Charles de Gaulle','The Beatles'], l:['civil-rights','vietnam-war','second-wave-feminism'], k:['liberalism','conservatism','feminism']},

{id:'second-wave-feminism', y:1969, d:'1960s–1980s', r:'Worldwide', c:['movement'],
 t:'Second-wave feminism, gay liberation and the environmental movement',
 s:'New movements fight for women\'s equality, gay rights and the protection of the planet, transforming laws and attitudes.',
 b:`After winning the vote, women in most countries still faced unequal pay, exclusion from many jobs, limited reproductive rights and legal subordination in marriage. In 1949 the French philosopher Simone de Beauvoir published "The Second Sex", arguing "one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman": that women's roles were shaped by society, not nature. In 1963 Betty Friedan's "The Feminine Mystique" described the frustration of American housewives. A "second wave" of feminism campaigned for equal pay, access to contraception and abortion, an end to workplace discrimination, and action against domestic violence and rape. Many countries passed equal pay and anti-discrimination laws, and women entered universities and professions in large numbers.

In June 1969, police raided the Stonewall Inn, a gay bar in New York; the patrons fought back, sparking days of protest. Stonewall became the symbol of the gay liberation movement. Homosexuality, which had been a crime in most countries (Alan Turing, the British codebreaking genius, was prosecuted for it), was gradually decriminalised, and in later decades many countries legalised same-sex marriage, starting with the Netherlands in 2001.

Rachel Carson's "Silent Spring" (1962) revealed how pesticides like DDT were killing wildlife and launched the modern environmental movement. The first Earth Day (1970) drew 20 million Americans. Governments created environmental agencies and passed clean air and water laws. In 1987 the Montreal Protocol successfully banned chemicals destroying the ozone layer, a rare global environmental success.`,
 m:'These movements changed laws and attitudes about gender, sexuality and nature, and the environmental movement laid the groundwork for the fight against climate change.',
 p:['Simone de Beauvoir','Betty Friedan','Gloria Steinem','Marsha P. Johnson','Harvey Milk','Rachel Carson'], l:['womens-suffrage','civil-rights','sixty-eight','climate-change'], k:['feminism','human-rights','liberalism']},

{id:'cambodia-genocide', y:1975, d:'1975–1979', r:'Cambodia', c:['revolution','disaster'],
 t:'The Khmer Rouge and the Cambodian genocide',
 s:'Pol Pot\'s communist Khmer Rouge empties Cambodia\'s cities and kills about a quarter of the population.',
 b:`Cambodia was drawn into the Vietnam War: the US secretly bombed the country heavily to destroy North Vietnamese supply routes, and a US-backed general overthrew Prince Sihanouk in 1970. A civil war followed. In April 1975 the Khmer Rouge ("Red Khmers"), a radical communist movement led by Pol Pot, captured the capital, Phnom Penh.

The Khmer Rouge set out to create a pure, classless, agrarian communist society starting from "Year Zero". Within days, they forced the entire population of the cities, about two million people, into the countryside to work on collective farms. Money, markets, schools, religion and private property were abolished. Families were separated. Anyone considered an enemy, including former officials, intellectuals, teachers, monks, ethnic minorities such as Vietnamese and Cham Muslims, and even people who wore glasses or spoke a foreign language, was killed. At the Tuol Sleng prison (S-21), some 14,000–20,000 people were tortured into false confessions and executed; only a handful survived.

Between 1975 and 1979, about 1.5 to 2 million people, roughly a quarter of Cambodia's population, died from execution, starvation, overwork and disease. The mass graves became known as the "Killing Fields". In 1979 Vietnam invaded and drove out the Khmer Rouge, who fled to the jungle; shamefully, Western countries and China continued to recognise the Khmer Rouge at the UN for years because they opposed Vietnam. A UN-backed tribunal finally convicted a few surviving leaders of genocide decades later.`,
 m:'The Cambodian genocide is a terrible example of where utopian ideology and absolute power can lead when all restraints are removed.',
 p:['Pol Pot','Ieng Sary','Kang Kek Iew ("Duch")','Norodom Sihanouk'], l:['vietnam-war','mao-china','angkor'], k:['genocide','communism','totalitarianism']},

{id:'deng-reforms', y:1978, d:'1978–1992', r:'China', c:['economy','revolution'],
 t:'Deng Xiaoping\'s reforms and Tiananmen Square',
 s:'China\'s new leader opens the country to markets and foreign investment, beginning its economic miracle, but crushes calls for democracy.',
 b:`After Mao's death in 1976, Deng Xiaoping, a veteran revolutionary purged twice under Mao, became China's paramount leader. He was a pragmatist: "It doesn't matter whether a cat is black or white, as long as it catches mice." From 1978 he launched "Reform and Opening Up".

Collective farms were dismantled and peasants were allowed to sell surplus crops for profit; food production soared. "Special Economic Zones" like Shenzhen, then a fishing village, opened to foreign investment and became giant manufacturing cities. Private businesses were allowed. China became the "workshop of the world", making toys, clothes and later electronics for global markets. Over the following decades, its economy grew at about 10% a year, and some 800 million people rose out of extreme poverty, the greatest poverty reduction in history. To limit population growth, the government imposed a strict "one-child policy" (1980–2015), enforced at times with forced abortions and sterilisations.

But Deng would not allow political freedom. In spring 1989, students and workers gathered in Beijing's Tiananmen Square, demanding democracy, free speech and an end to corruption; they built a "Goddess of Democracy" statue. On the night of 3–4 June, the army moved in with tanks and gunfire, killing hundreds, possibly thousands. An unknown man standing in front of a column of tanks became one of the most famous images of the century. The massacre remains censored in China. Deng's formula of economic freedom with Communist Party political control continues to define China.`,
 m:'Deng\'s reforms set China on the path to becoming the world\'s second-largest economy, transforming the global economy, while Tiananmen confirmed the Party\'s monopoly on power.',
 p:['Deng Xiaoping','Hu Yaobang','Zhao Ziyang','"Tank Man"'], l:['mao-china','rise-of-china','fall-of-berlin-wall'], k:['communism','capitalism','globalization','democracy']},

{id:'iranian-revolution', y:1979, d:'1953–1989', r:'Iran', c:['revolution','idea'],
 t:'The Iranian Revolution',
 s:'A popular revolution overthrows the Shah of Iran and creates an Islamic Republic led by Ayatollah Khomeini.',
 b:`In 1953 the CIA and British intelligence organised a coup that overthrew Iran's elected prime minister, Mohammad Mossadegh, who had nationalised the oil industry. Power returned to the monarch, Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi. The Shah, backed by the US, pursued rapid modernisation and Westernisation, including land reform and women's rights, but ruled as a dictator with a feared secret police, SAVAK. Oil wealth flowed to an elite, while many Iranians felt their culture and religion were being trampled.

Opposition united liberals, leftists and, above all, Shia Muslim clergy. The most important figure was Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini, a cleric exiled in 1964 whose sermons were smuggled into Iran on cassette tapes. In 1978 massive demonstrations and strikes paralysed the country. In January 1979 the Shah fled, and in February Khomeini returned to a rapturous welcome by millions. Iran became an "Islamic Republic" based on Khomeini's doctrine of "guardianship of the Islamic jurist": ultimate authority rests with a senior cleric, the Supreme Leader. Islamists then turned on their former allies, and leftists and liberals were jailed or executed. Islamic dress codes were imposed on women.

In November 1979, students seized the US embassy in Tehran and held 52 Americans hostage for 444 days. In 1980 Iraq's dictator Saddam Hussein invaded Iran; the Iran-Iraq War (1980–88) killed perhaps half a million or more, with Iraq using chemical weapons. The revolution made Iran a leading anti-American and anti-Israeli power, supporting groups such as Hezbollah in Lebanon, and inspired Islamist movements worldwide.`,
 m:'The revolution created a theocratic state that has been a central player in Middle Eastern conflicts ever since and showed that religion could be a powerful revolutionary force in modern politics.',
 p:['Mohammad Mossadegh','Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi','Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini','Saddam Hussein'], l:['sunni-shia','safavid-mughal','six-day-war','soviet-afghan-war','nine-eleven'], k:['islam','secularism','monarchy']},

{id:'soviet-afghan-war', y:1979.9, d:'1979–1989', r:'Afghanistan', c:['war'],
 t:'The Soviet war in Afghanistan',
 s:'The USSR invades Afghanistan to prop up a communist government; US-backed mujahideen fight a decade-long guerrilla war.',
 b:`In 1978 a communist party seized power in Afghanistan and tried to impose radical reforms on a deeply conservative, mostly rural Muslim society, provoking uprisings. In December 1979 the Soviet Union invaded to save the regime.

Afghan resistance fighters, called "mujahideen" (those who wage jihad), fought the Soviets from the mountains. The US, through the CIA, together with Saudi Arabia and Pakistan, poured billions of dollars of weapons into the resistance, including, from 1986, shoulder-fired Stinger missiles that shot down Soviet helicopters. Thousands of foreign Muslim volunteers, including a wealthy Saudi named Osama bin Laden, came to fight. About a million Afghans died and over five million fled as refugees to Pakistan and Iran.

The war became the Soviet Union's "Vietnam". About 15,000 Soviet soldiers died, the costs drained the economy, and it damaged morale and the regime's legitimacy. Mikhail Gorbachev withdrew Soviet forces in 1989. But Afghanistan did not find peace: the mujahideen factions fought a civil war, and in 1996 the Taliban, a strict Islamist movement, took Kabul. Bin Laden's network, al-Qaeda, founded in 1988, found a base there, from which it would plan the 9/11 attacks.`,
 m:'The war weakened the Soviet Union, contributing to its collapse, and the jihadist networks it nurtured later turned against the West.',
 p:['Leonid Brezhnev','Mikhail Gorbachev','Ahmad Shah Massoud','Osama bin Laden','Ronald Reagan'], l:['iranian-revolution','soviet-collapse','nine-eleven'], k:['communism','terrorism','islam','geopolitics']},

{id:'gulf-war', y:1990.6, d:'1990–1991', r:'Kuwait & Iraq', c:['war'],
 t:'The Gulf War',
 s:'Iraq invades Kuwait; a US-led coalition authorised by the UN drives it out in a short, high-tech war.',
 b:`In August 1990, Iraq's dictator Saddam Hussein, deep in debt after his war with Iran, invaded and annexed neighbouring Kuwait, a small, oil-rich state. He accused it of stealing Iraqi oil and keeping oil prices low. Saddam now controlled about 20% of the world's oil reserves and threatened Saudi Arabia.

With the Cold War ending, the Soviet Union did not block action at the UN, and the Security Council authorised force to liberate Kuwait. US President George H.W. Bush assembled a coalition of 35 countries, including Arab states like Saudi Arabia, Egypt and Syria. In January 1991, Operation Desert Storm began with weeks of precision bombing broadcast live on television, with "smart bombs" and cruise missiles. A ground offensive in February liberated Kuwait in about 100 hours. Retreating Iraqi forces set Kuwait's oil wells on fire.

The coalition did not march on Baghdad, and Saddam stayed in power. When Shia Muslims in the south and Kurds in the north rebelled, encouraged by the US, Saddam crushed them brutally. The UN imposed strict sanctions on Iraq that devastated its population. The presence of US troops in Saudi Arabia, home of Islam's holiest sites, became one of Osama bin Laden's main grievances.`,
 m:'The Gulf War showcased US military dominance in the post-Cold War world and set up the unfinished business that led to the 2003 Iraq War.',
 p:['Saddam Hussein','George H.W. Bush','Colin Powell','Norman Schwarzkopf'], l:['iranian-revolution','nine-eleven','iraq-war'], k:['sovereignty','geopolitics']},

{id:'fall-of-berlin-wall', y:1989.85, d:'1985–1990', r:'Eastern Europe', c:['revolution','movement'],
 t:'The fall of the Berlin Wall and the revolutions of 1989',
 s:'Peaceful revolutions sweep away communist rule across Eastern Europe; the Berlin Wall falls and Germany reunites.',
 b:`In 1961 East Germany had built the Berlin Wall to stop its citizens from fleeing to the West through West Berlin; around 140 people were killed trying to cross it. Earlier uprisings in East Germany (1953), Hungary (1956) and Czechoslovakia (1968) had been crushed by Soviet tanks.

In 1985 Mikhail Gorbachev became leader of the Soviet Union. Facing a stagnant economy, he launched "perestroika" (restructuring) and "glasnost" (openness), allowing criticism and freer media. Crucially, he made clear the USSR would no longer use force to keep Eastern European regimes in power. In Poland, the independent trade union "Solidarity", led by shipyard electrician Lech Wałęsa and supported by the Polish Pope John Paul II, had been banned since 1981; in 1989 the government was forced to negotiate, and Solidarity won partly free elections in June. Hungary opened its border with Austria, and thousands of East Germans fled through it.

East Germans took to the streets in huge peaceful demonstrations, chanting "We are the people!" On the evening of 9 November 1989, after a bungled announcement by an official, crowds surged to the checkpoints, and the overwhelmed guards opened the gates. Berliners climbed onto the Wall and began chipping it apart. Communist regimes then fell in Czechoslovakia (the peaceful "Velvet Revolution" led by playwright Václav Havel), Bulgaria and, violently, Romania, where dictator Nicolae Ceaușescu was executed. Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990.`,
 m:'1989 ended the division of Europe, freed hundreds of millions of people from communist dictatorship, and showed the power of peaceful mass protest.',
 p:['Mikhail Gorbachev','Lech Wałęsa','Pope John Paul II','Václav Havel','Helmut Kohl','Ronald Reagan'], l:['cold-war-begins','soviet-collapse','european-integration','deng-reforms'], k:['communism','democracy','nationalism']},

{id:'soviet-collapse', y:1991.5, d:'1991', r:'Soviet Union', c:['revolution','empire'],
 t:'The collapse of the Soviet Union',
 s:'After a failed hardline coup, the Soviet Union dissolves into fifteen independent countries, ending the Cold War.',
 b:`Gorbachev's reforms unleashed forces he could not control. The economy worsened, with shortages and long queues. Glasnost allowed people to speak openly about Stalin's crimes, undermining faith in the system. The 1986 Chernobyl nuclear disaster in Ukraine, initially covered up, damaged trust further. Above all, the many nations inside the USSR, from the Baltic states to Ukraine, Georgia and Central Asia, demanded independence. In 1989 two million people in Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania joined hands in a 600-km human chain, the "Baltic Way".

In August 1991, hardline communists placed Gorbachev under house arrest at his holiday home in Crimea and tried to seize power. In Moscow, Boris Yeltsin, the elected president of the Russian republic, climbed onto a tank outside the parliament and called for resistance. Crowds defended the building, soldiers refused to fire, and the coup collapsed within three days. It fatally discredited the Communist Party.

Republic after republic declared independence. In December 1991 the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus declared that the Soviet Union "ceases to exist". On 25 December 1991, Gorbachev resigned, and the red Soviet flag was lowered over the Kremlin for the last time. Fifteen independent states emerged, including Russia, Ukraine, Belarus, Kazakhstan, Georgia and the Baltic states. The Cold War was over, and the US was left as the world's only superpower. In Russia, the 1990s brought economic chaos, a collapse in living standards and the rise of super-rich "oligarchs", experiences that shaped Vladimir Putin's later rise.`,
 m:'The end of the Soviet Union ended the Cold War and redrew the map of Eurasia; Russia\'s sense of loss over it remains a key driver of its foreign policy today.',
 p:['Mikhail Gorbachev','Boris Yeltsin','Leonid Kravchuk','Vladimir Putin (later)'], l:['fall-of-berlin-wall','soviet-afghan-war','russian-revolution','ukraine-war'], k:['communism','nationalism','sovereignty']}
]);
